====Adverb====
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[perchance|Perchance]]; [[perhaps]].

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|chancen}}, {{m|enm|chauncen}}, from the noun (see above).

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|archaic|intransitive}} To [[happen]] by chance, to [[occur]].
#: {{ux|en|It '''chanced''' that I found a solution the very next day.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Deuteronomy|22|6|text=if a bird's nest '''chance''' to be before thee}}
#* {{RQ:Carlyle Past and Present|2|ch. XV, Practical — Devotional||Once {{...}} it '''chanced''' that Geoffrey Riddell {{w|Bishop of Ely}}, a Prelate rather troublesome to {{w|Samson of Tottington|our Abbot}}, made a request of him for timber from his woods towards certain edifices going on at {{w|Glemsford}}.}}
#* {{RQ:Charlotte Bronte Jane Eyre|chapter=XVIII|text=Mr. Mason, shivering as some one '''chanced''' to open the door, asked for more coal to be put on the fire, which had burnt out its flame, though its mass of cinder still shone hot and red. The footman who brought the coal, in going out, stopped near Mr. Eshton's chair, and said something to him in a low voice, of which I heard only the words, "old woman,"—"quite troublesome."}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|transitive}} To [[befall]]; to [[happen]] to.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1826|author=William Lambarde|title=A Perambulation of Kent|passage={{...}} while the King and Godwine sate at the table, accompanied with others of the nobilitie, it '''chanced''' the cupbearer (as he brought wine to the bourd) to slip with the one foote, and yet by good strength of his other leg, to recover himselfe without falling {{...}}}}
# To [[try]] or [[risk]].
#: {{ux|en|Shall we carry the umbrella, or '''chance''' a rainstorm?}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1890|author=w:William Dean Howells|title=A Hazard of New Fortunes
|passage=He does '''chance''' it in stocks, but he's always played on the square, if you call stocks gambling.}}
# To [[discover]] something by chance.
#: {{ux|en|He '''chanced''' upon a kindly stranger who showed him the way.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|V|i|passage=I '''chanced''' on this letter.}}
# {{lb|en|Belize}} To [[rob]], [[cheat]] or [[swindle]] someone.
#: {{ux|en|The car broke down a week after I bought it. I was '''chanced''' by that fast-talking salesman.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2017-03-22|author=Jules Vasquez|work=7 News Belize|title=Shyne Urges Artists To Protest Against Businesses Countrywide|url=http://www.7newsbelize.com/sstory.php?nid=39763|text=Be prepared to engage in protests of all businesses nationwide who are violating the copyright act and '''chancing''' our members.}}
# {{lb|en|Nigeria}} To [[take]] an [[opportunity]] from someone; to [[cut]] a [[queue]].

